# Line endings are LF, on every platform, checkout included.
#
# Not a style preference — three of the checks a site inherits from this
# template work by regenerating a file and diffing it (vercel.json, src/content,
# public/editor-*.css), and the generators are Node scripts that write LF.
#
# Git for Windows ships core.autocrlf=true, so a fresh clone writes CRLF into
# the working tree while the blobs stay LF. The very first `npm run headers` or
# `npm run editor` then rewrites those files as LF, and git reports all three as
# modified while `git diff` shows nothing at all:
#
#     $ npm run editor && git status --porcelain -- public/
#      M public/editor-inline.css
#      M public/editor-panel.css
#     $ git diff -- public/
#     warning: LF will be replaced by CRLF in public/editor-inline.css
#     (no diff)
#
# That state is genuinely hard to read — measured on a fresh clone of this
# template on 2026-08-06 — and the instinct it teaches ("commit whatever
# appeared") is the wrong one to learn on a repo whose CI diffs generated files.
# It bites hardest on a site driven by an agent, where a generated-files gate
# goes red on session one for a reason no one can see in a diff.
#
# eol=lf makes the checkout match what the generators produce, so the gates
# behave the same on a Windows laptop as they do on CI's ubuntu-latest.
#
# Inherited by every site created from this template, which is the point:
# sk-studio hit this, solved it, and documented it, and every new site was
# repeating the discovery.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary, byte for byte.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.mp4 binary
*.pdf binary
